{"id":"billowy_smoke_plume","compound_name":"Graphs::Aero::billowy_smoke_plume","source_path":"billowy_smoke_plume/billowy_smoke_plume.json","category":"Smoke","author":"Autodesk","description":"An aero simulation graph producing a billowy smoke plume","documentation_file":"billowy_smoke_plume_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"## Billowy Smoke Graph\n\n### What it does\n\nThis graph uses aero to create a billowy smoke simulation, perfect for chimneys and any plume of smoke.\n\n### Inputs and outputs \n\nInputs for this graph are the meshes you wish to emit smoke from.  The output is the resulting aero volume from the smoke simulation.  \n\n### Useful information\n\n- This graph relies on variations in `fog_density` and `temperature` at sourcing time in order to produce a more detailed and dynamic plume of smoke.  Higher source temperatures will make the plume rise faster.  For an extra initial push, `initial speed` is used. It also uses fluffy `style` (cubic transport interpolation) on the solver settings, to produce the billowy look.\n\n- Performance is directly related to the `fluid detail size` on the source air node, and the height of the desired plume.  Dissipation will determine how long the plume lasts and thus its height.  Further optimizations can be made by using the adaptivity node.\n\n- The speed of movement for the plume is directly related to scene scale. Smaller objects emit faster moving smoke, whereas large objects produce bigger masses of smoke which are slower to move.  Please adjust the `scene units in meters` on the solver settings appropriately, or scale your geometry.\n\n### Example scene\n\n- The example scenes provides an example of how this effect works great for a rooftop industrial chimney.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.15.0.9","bif":"2.15.0.9","fields":"2.15.0.9","file":"2.15.0.9","fluids":"2.15.0.9","geometries":"2.15.0.9","simulation":"2.15.0.9"},"graph_ports":[{"portName":"out_objects1","portDirection":"output","portType":"array<Obj